# GitHub API Flutter App — Instructions & Rules

Instructions for working on this project and rules for code style, structure, and contributions.

---

## 1. Project overview

- **Name:** `github_api`
- **Type:** Flutter mobile app (Android / iOS)
- **Purpose:** Browse GitHub users, repositories, and events via the GitHub API
- **SDK:** Dart `^3.7.2`
- **Key dependencies:** `http`, `timeago`, `url_launcher`

---

## 2. Getting started

### Prerequisites

- Flutter SDK (compatible with Dart 3.7.2+)
- Android Studio / Xcode for device/simulator (as needed)

### Commands

```bash
# Install dependencies
flutter pub get

# Run app (connected device or emulator)
flutter run

# Run analyzer (lint)
flutter analyze

# Run tests
flutter test
```

### Project layout

- `lib/` — Application source
  - `main.dart` — App entry point
  - `models/` — Data models (e.g. `github_user`, `github_repo`, `github_event`)
  - `pages/` — Full-screen pages (e.g. user details, repo details)
  - `services/` — API and business logic (e.g. `github_service`)
  - `utils/` — Helpers (e.g. errors, time formatting)
  - `widgets/` — Reusable UI components
- `test/` — Unit and widget tests
- `android/`, `ios/` — Platform-specific code

---

## 3. Code rules and conventions

### 3.1 Dart & Flutter style

- Follow [Effective Dart](https://dart.dev/guides/language/effective-dart) (style, documentation, usage).
- Use the project’s `analysis_options.yaml` and run `flutter analyze` before committing; fix all analyzer errors and warnings.
- Prefer `const` constructors and widgets where possible.
- Use single quotes for strings unless the string contains a single quote.
- Format with `dart format` (or your IDE’s formatter).

### 3.2 Naming

- **Files:** `snake_case` (e.g. `repo_details_page.dart`, `github_service.dart`).
- **Classes / enums / typedefs:** `PascalCase`.
- **Variables, parameters, functions, methods:** `camelCase`.
- **Private members:** leading underscore, `_camelCase`.
- **Constants:** `camelCase` or `lowerCamelCase` (Dart convention).

### 3.3 Project structure rules

- **Models** (`lib/models/`): Plain data classes; use `fromJson`/`toJson` for API parsing. Keep free of UI and HTTP logic.
- **Services** (`lib/services/`): API calls and app-level logic only. No direct use of Flutter widgets; return data or throw/handle errors.
- **Pages** (`lib/pages/`): One main widget per file; file name should match the primary page/screen (e.g. `user_details_page.dart` → `UserDetailsPage`).
- **Widgets** (`lib/widgets/`): Reusable, presentational or small feature widgets. Prefer small, focused widgets over large monolithic ones.
- **Utils** (`lib/utils/`): Pure helpers (formatting, error types, etc.) with no dependency on Flutter UI or HTTP.

### 3.4 API and external usage

- All GitHub API access must go through `lib/services/github_service.dart` (or an agreed single service layer). Do not scatter raw `http` calls across pages/widgets.
- Handle errors via `lib/utils/app_errors.dart` (or the project’s chosen error types). Show user-friendly messages; log or report technical details as needed.
- Do not commit API keys or secrets. Use environment variables or a secure config approach for any tokens.

### 3.5 UI and UX

- Use Material Design (or the project’s chosen design system) consistently.
- Support different screen sizes and orientations where relevant.
- Show loading and error states for all async operations (e.g. list loading, pull-to-refresh, error retry).

---

## 4. Testing

- Add or update tests when adding or changing behavior, especially in services and reusable widgets.
- Run `flutter test` before pushing. Keep tests in `test/` and name them so it’s clear what they cover.

---

## 5. Version control and workflow

- Commit often with clear, short messages (e.g. “Add repo details page”, “Fix user search error handling”).
- Do not commit generated or local-only files (e.g. `build/`, `.dart_tool/`, IDE config that’s not shared). Respect `.gitignore`.
- Before opening a PR or pushing to a shared branch, run `flutter analyze` and `flutter test`.

---

## 6. Adding dependencies

- Prefer packages from [pub.dev](https://pub.dev) with good maintenance and compatibility with Dart 3.x.
- Add version constraints in `pubspec.yaml` (e.g. `^1.2.0`). After adding or changing dependencies, run `flutter pub get` and `flutter analyze`.
- Document in code or in this file why a new dependency is needed if it’s not obvious.

---

## 7. Summary checklist

Before considering a change “done”:

- [ ] `flutter pub get` (if dependencies changed)
- [ ] `flutter analyze` passes
- [ ] `flutter test` passes
- [ ] No hardcoded secrets or API keys
- [ ] New code follows the structure (models / services / pages / widgets / utils) and naming above
- [ ] User-facing errors and loading states are handled

